  "expected": "Loving One, Maitreya, with Asanga,",
  "evidence": "The Tibetan quoted in this slot is `byams pa thogs med`. TCS14:65 settles it inside this same corpus: the wylie `'di ni rje btsun byams pa nas thogs med dang, rje btsun 'jam dbyangs nas klu sgrub dang` is rendered \"those passed down to Master Asanga from the holy Maitreya, those come to the realized being Nagarjuna from the holy Manjushri\" - byams pa = Maitreya, thogs med = Asanga, in exactly this pairing, and immediately followed by the 'jam dbyangs pairing that C03:300 also continues in its next clause. TCS17:552 supplies the identical appositive: wylie `byams chen gyi mdun du phebs nas` is rendered \"The Lama came before the great image of the Loving One, Maitreya\". C01:471 renders `mgon po byams pa` as \"the savior Loving One\" and `'jam dbyangs` as \"Gentle Voice\", so \"Loving One\" is GMR's own English for byams pa and the Sanskrit appositive after it must be Maitreya. Avalokiteshvara is spyan ras gzigs, which this corpus renders \"Loving Eyes\": TITLS:817, wylie `rje btsun spyan ras gzigs la phyag 'tshal ba`, English \"An Obeisance to the Revered Loving Eyes [Avalokiteshvara]\". The parallel item four positions later, `'jam dbyangs` -> \"Gentle Voice, Manjushri\", establishes the pattern as [English of the Tibetan name], [Sanskrit of the same figure], so the slot cannot hold a different deity. All four cited rows were re-read from the spine for this report. Per rule 10 the English stands unchanged on the page and \"Avalokiteshvara,\" is left unwrapped; s09 keys byams pa to \"Loving One\" only.",
