  "expected": "rnams shlooo ka re lhan du gsungs",
  "evidence": "The wylie conversion drops the ACIP Sanskrit brace code {SHl} (sh with subjoined la), leaving the orphan `ooo ka`, which cannot be a Tibetan syllable sequence - no consonant, and a tripled vowel letter. The acip field of this same row preserves the source: \"SKYE BA KUN TU RNAMS {SHl}OOO KA RE LHAN DU GSUNGS,\". Measured over the whole spine: 23 rows carry a {SHl} brace code and all 23 lose the `shl` in their wylie (count of rows where acip matches '%{SHl}OO%' and wylie matches '%oo ka%' but not '%shl%' = 23 of 23); by contrast 9 rows spell the word without the brace code as SHLOO KA and all 9 keep it, wylie `shloo ka` (e.g. TITLS:1252 acip \"...POD SO SOR SHLOO KA RE\" -> wylie \"...pod so sor shloo ka re\"; also C03:349 and C07:80). Two of the damaged rows are in this same document: C03:302 acip \"LHA YI SKAD DANG SOGS {SHl}OO KA RE RE\" -> wylie \"lha yi skad dang sogs oo ka re re\"; C03:378 acip \"ZHES PA'I {SHl}OO KA GCIG STE\" -> wylie \"zhes pa'i oo ka gcig ste\". The word is Sanskrit shloka, \"verse\", which is what the English of this segment renders (\"the various verses\"). `expected` above is the converter-faithful output for this row's source as keyed; the residual tripled vowel is a separate source defect, filed below, after which the reading is `shloo ka`. Effect: the word is unsearchable and unreadable in the wylie layer in all 23 rows.",
