  "evidence": "The word is Sanskrit shloka, 'verse' (the English of this segment renders the phrase 'bespoke the verses'), and 'oo' cannot be a Tibetan syllable — it has no root letter, so it will not convert to unicode and will not be found by any syllable-keyed lookup. Verified in the spine: C03:349 and C16:414 carry the SAME Tibetan sentence word for word — 'dang po ni, ,yon tan kun gzhir gyur drin can rje, ,tshul bzhin bsten pa lam gyi rtsa ba ru, ,legs par mthong nas 'bad pa du ma yis, ,gus pa chen pos bsten par byin gyis rlobs, ,zhes pa'i X ka gcig gis bstan' — where C03:349 reads 'shloo ka' and C16:414 reads 'oo ka'. That minimal pair fixes the full form and proves the two spellings are the same word. Corpus-wide the split is: 9 segments with 'shloo ka' (C03:349, C07:80, TITLS:847, 1252, 1282, 1289, 2514, 2570, 2597), 1 with 'shlo ka' (ILL:2047), and 22 with the bare 'oo ka' (C03:302, 378, 403, 432, 463, 478, 495, 505, 515, 524, 546, 560, 574; C16:414, 450, 463, 479, 482, 484, 492, 499, 507). Both forms occur inside C03, so this is not a per-file ingest failure dropping the 'shl' cluster; it reads as inconsistency carried in the source e-text. Adjacent but NOT claimed here: C03:300 has a third form, 'rnams ooo ka re lhan du gsungs', with three o's — outside this segment, flagged only so whoever aligns 300 checks it.",
