  "evidence": "This segment's own acip column reads \"SLOB DPON TZA{ndr} GO MIS,\" — the {ndr} braced cluster (Sanskrit ndra) is present in the ACIP and absent from the wylie, so the ACIP-to-wylie conversion dropped it. This segment's English names the author \"the Master Chandragomi\", which requires the ndra. Other witnesses in the spine spell the name intact: EM:1022 \"slob dpon tsandra goo mis\" and ILL:164 \"tsandra go mi\" (the go/goo alternation is genuine orthographic variation and is NOT part of this finding — only the missing ndra is). The defect is systematic, not a one-off slip: exactly three segments corpus-wide carry {ndr} in their ACIP (C03:312, C16:397, C09:49) and all three lose it in the wylie — C09:49 turns ACIP \"MU N'AI {ndr}A M{NYDZ}A{u2}\" into wylie \"mu nAi a ma{u2}\", dropping {ndr} and {NYDZ} while the {u2} escape survives, so braced consonant-stack escapes specifically are being discarded rather than expanded. Unbraced clusters convert correctly (TCS15:7 ACIP \"TZAndA LI'I\" -> wylie \"tsaNDa li'i\"), which isolates the fault to the curly-brace form. C16:397 carries this segment with identical wylie and acip (its English differs only in lacking the footnote digit), so the same repair applies there.",
