  "evidence": "'rtsva' is not valid EWTS — wa-zur is written 'w' (rtswa, རྩྭ). The ACIP column of this segment reads RTZVA, which is CORRECT ACIP (V is the wa-zur code), so the defect is in the derived wylie column, not in the ACIP. Proved with the project's canonical engines, run just now: engines/hgm_tools.acip_to_ewts('RTZVA') returns 'rtsva' (V passed through unmapped, so not an EWTS letter) while acip_to_ewts('RTZWA') returns 'rtswa'; engines/ewts_unicode.wylie_to_unicode('rtsva') returns the failure marker ('⟨rtsva⟩', False) whereas wylie_to_unicode('rtswa') returns ('རྩྭ', True). So this word simply does not render in Tibetan here. P1:9 carries this identical verse and its wylie reads 'rtswa kham 'ga'' (its ACIP happens to read RTZWA), so the same verse renders there and not here. Not a local slip: the wylie column spells 'rtsva' in 21 spine segments (C03:144, C03:313, C05:397, C08:339, C10:386, C10:387, C16:398, C16:913, C17:428, C17:722, TCS03:214, TCS10:569, TCS11:372, TCS11:373, TCS13:245, TCS17:561, GIE:142, GIE:201, AK:442, ILL:4214, OSE:217) against 2 segments spelling 'rtswa', and 1,885 segments carry a literal 'v' somewhere in the wylie (e.g. 'dvang', 'bkvan'), which is not an EWTS letter at all. All counts are segment counts and are therefore lower bounds on occurrences.",
