  "expected": "de bde skyes bu'i rtsal de 'phags",
  "evidence": "This same segment spells the word correctly about thirty words earlier, in \"'di na skyes bu gzhan don byed par\" — an internal inconsistency inside one segment. Corpus-wide the wylie column has 'skyes bu' in 374 spine segments against 'skyes pu' in 7 (segment counts, so lower bounds): C03:313, C16:398 and P1:9, which are three witnesses of this one verse and share an ACIP lineage rather than being independent; C03:332, C16:411 and P1:22, likewise one verse in three witnesses, reading 'skyes pu chen po'i' where the standard form is 'skyes bu chen po'i'; and TCS12:64 'skyes pu dam pa', standardly 'skyes bu dam pa'. Both spellings convert cleanly to unicode (སྐྱེས་པུའི vs སྐྱེས་བུའི), so the cost is not rendering but keying and search against a headword that appears in 374 other segments. CAVEAT to record with any fix: GMR's English here is \"This is a park of pleasure\", which renders neither 'skyes pu'i rtsal' nor 'skyes bu'i rtsal' — rtsal (རྩལ) is skill/prowess, not a park, and the near-homophone tshal (ཚལ) is grove/park — so he was very likely reading a pleasure-garden term. If so the corruption is wider than the b/p and the phrase needs a witness edition before anything is keyed to it; no spine witness carries such a reading, which is why spans s41 and s42 are nulled and no separate erratum is filed for the grove word.",
  "severity": "MEDIUM",
  "confidence": "PROBABLE",
  "note": "filed from the alignment batch, spine-verified on merge"
 },
 {
