  "evidence": "C03:317's full Wylie is \"de'i phyir de dag la drin gzo'i bsam pa med na, klu'i rgyal po rnga sgra'i tshigs su bcad pa las\" — it runs straight from the conditional clause to the citation frame and contains no term for 'evil', 'way/custom', 'conscience' or 'shame'. Every lexical correspondent sits instead in C03:320, whose Wylie opens \"zhes pa ltar ma rabs khrel med kyi lugs ngan pa yin pas\" (ma rabs = a base/low person, khrel med = without shame or conscience, lugs ngan pa = an evil way or custom). C03:320's own English begins \"Think: everything we have now, from this precious human body on up\" and renders nothing of that opening clause. So the sentence is present exactly where its Tibetan is not, and absent exactly where its Tibetan is. CAVEAT, which is why this is filed low and probable rather than confirmed: the displacement spans the two quotations at C03:318 and C03:319, and 320's clause is governed by the quotation-closing formula 'zhes pa ltar'. GMR may therefore have deliberately front-loaded the summing-up sentence ahead of the verses he was about to quote, in which case the corpus faithfully records the printed order and this is a translation-order fact rather than an ingest slip. Either way the practical consequence for span work is the same: no Tibetan in 317 may be keyed to this sentence, and it is left wholly unwrapped under rule 10.",
