  "evidence": "After a final -g the nominalizer takes the pa/par form, not ba/bar (standard 'jug pa, 'jug par). The sense here is unambiguously 'wishing to enter the door', so the alternative reading of bar as the separate noun 'interval / up to' is excluded. Spine check, span-level: exactly five segments contain the string 'jug bar, and I inspected all five — three (C03:367, C16:431, TCS11:199) are zung 'jug bar gyi, where bar IS the noun 'up to', and the remaining two are this sentence, duplicated verbatim at C16:412. So the nominalizer reading of bar after -g occurs nowhere else in the spine. The same orthographic rule holds elsewhere: all twelve thob bar segments are ma ... thob bar du ('until'), the noun again. By contrast 'jug par appears in 151 segments (a segment count, i.e. an upper bound on distinct spans, not a span-level tally). The ACIP column of this segment reads 'JUG BAR as well, so the deviation sits in the transcription source, not in spine ingest. Not an instance of any registered systematic class (no footnote fusion, folio marker, or glued-on heading). No span keys to the defect: bar is nulled on independent grounds.",
