  "evidence": "Verified independently against the shipped spine. (1) FREQUENCY: the substring 'spyar' occurs in exactly ONE of the 42,199 corpus segments - this one; 'sbyar' occurs in 667, and the specific idiom 'mtshams sbyar' in 35 (C01:486, C03:437, C05:36, C05:97, C06:32, C08:128, C09:205, C16:275, C16:685, C17:304, C17:514, TCS01:206, TCS01:331, TCS03:231, TCS10:629, TCS11:16, TCS11:60, TCS14:476, TCS16Reading:128, TCS18Commentary:28, and 15 more). (2) LEXICON: the 105,634-entry dictionary carries 'sbyar', 'sbyar ba', 'sbyar bar bya', 'mtshams sbyar' and 'mtshams sbyar ba'; a query for entries matching 'spyar%' or '%mtshams spyar%' returns ZERO. (3) DIRECT PARALLEL: TCS01:206 is the same three-clause formula almost verbatim - 'tshul khrims rnam par dag pas gzhi bzung, sbyin sogs kyis grogs byas, smon lam dri ma med pas mtshams sbyar ba sogs dgos na' - and reads sbyar. (4) NOT AN INGEST ARTEFACT: the acip field of this segment independently reads SPYAR (the only segment in the corpus whose acip contains SPYAR), so wylie and ACIP agree and the defect is upstream in the source e-text, not in conversion. CONSEQUENCE: a Gofer or FTS search for the standard idiom mtshams sbyar silently misses this segment, and a lookup on spyar ba hits no dictionary entry. Banked VERBATIM at s30 per rule 1; the correction lives only here.",
