  "evidence": "\"oo ka\" is not a word in Tibetan or Sanskrit; the sense required is shloka (verse) — \"this is taught by these two verses\". This segment's own ACIP reads \",ZHES PA'I {SHl}OO KA GNYIS KYIS BSTAN TE,\": the braced Sanskrit-letter token {SHl} was dropped wholesale by the ACIP-to-Wylie converter. The defect tracks the BRACE NOTATION, not the reading — where the same word is keyed without braces the Wylie survives intact, verified in-spine at C03:349 (ACIP \"ZHES PA'I SHLOO KA GCIG GIS BSTAN,\" -> Wylie \"zhes pa'i shloo ka gcig gis bstan\"), C07:80 (ACIP \",ZHES PA'I SHLOO KA GCIG STE,\" -> Wylie \"zhes pa'i shloo ka gcig ste\"), and TITLS:847, 1252, 1282, 1289, 2514, 2570. Measured over the whole spine by this reconciler: 23 segments carry {SHl} in ACIP; 0 of the 23 retain \"shl\" anywhere in Wylie; exactly 22 segments have a bare \" oo ka\" in Wylie and all 22 are {SHl}OO KA segments; the 23rd is C03:300, whose ACIP \"{SHl}OOO KA\" became Wylie \"ooo ka\" — a complete 23/23 correspondence, so this is a systematic converter defect, not a local corruption. The 23 are C03:300, 302, 378, 403, 432, 463, 478, 495, 505, 515, 524, 546, 560, 574 and C16:414, 450, 463, 479, 482, 484, 492, 499, 507. PARALLEL-WITNESS CHECK PERFORMED: C16:450 carries the identical \"zhes pa'i oo ka gnyis kyis bstan te\", but its ACIP is also \"{SHl}OO KA\", so it is the same artefact propagated through the same converter, not an independent published reading. Consequence: the citation-count colophon does not match the dictionary and \"oo ka\" is unsearchable as a word.",
