  "evidence": "Two witnesses of the same published translation read plural, and this segment is the sole outlier. Verified directly against the spine: C16:450 (the parallel commentary citation) reads \"To stop the slightest Wrongs of many wrongs we do, And try t...\", and TCS08SourceOfAllMyGood:5 (the standalone prayer text, an independent line of transmission whose Tibetan is given in phonetics, not in this ACIP edition) likewise reads \"To stop the slightest Wrongs of many wrongs we do\". Only C03:403 has singular \"Wrong\". The Tibetan of this very line licenses the plural: \"phra zhing phra ba rnams kyang spong ba\" carries the plural \"rnams\" over the collective \"nyes pa'i tshogs\" of the preceding line. The C16 witness cannot be dismissed as converter-propagated the way the shloka artefact was — that artefact is a Wylie-side pipeline drop, whereas this is English text, and the TCS08 witness is a separate text entirely. The capital W is verse lineation, not apparatus, and is not the issue. NOTE ON A CONTRARY CLAIM: it is true that TCS08SourceOfAllMyGood:4 stops at \"As certain as my shadow\", but the line in question continues into TCS08SourceOfAllMyGood:5, which was checked and does carry it — so the \"no third witness\" objection does not hold.",
