  "evidence": "The figure is Pha Dam pa Sangs rgyas. 'sangs gyas' is not a Tibetan word — the ra-mgo of rgyas is missing, and bare 'gyas' means 'right side', which yields no reading here. The segment's own English reads 'Pa Dampa Sangye', confirming sangs rgyas is intended. Counts re-derived from the spine by me, not taken from either analyst: 'sangs rgyas' appears in 2,101 segments, 'sangs gyas' in exactly 2. 'pha dam pa' appears in only 4 segments corpus-wide: C03:410, P1:65, TCS03:369 ('pha dam pas'), and TCS12:146, which spells the same figure correctly — 'pha dam pa sangs rgyas kyi zhal nas'. That is an independent witness for the correct form in a different text. PARALLEL-WITNESS DISCLOSURE: P1:65 carries this same citation with the identical error (wylie 'ces pa dang pha dam pa sangs gyas kyis...', ACIP 'PHA DAM PA SANGS GYAS KYIS'), differing from C03:410 only in verse shad placement and the absence of the trailing footnote number. P1:65 is therefore not an independent witness but the same passage in a second course packet, sharing one ACIP lineage; it shows the defect is upstream of the C03 file rather than an ingest slip, and does not vindicate the spelling. I checked this against the register's own practice before filing: E-entries at C01:388 and C01:380 were banked CONFIRMED with the note that a parallel (C16:203, C16:199) read identically, 'so the defect is in the shared source text rather than a one-off slip', and the C03:313 entry files a defect while stating its parallels 'share an ACIP lineage rather than being independent'. The register's stated scope likewise says duplicate passages are named, not used to suppress. This entry follows that precedent. The defect is in the source keying, not in wylie derivation, since the ACIP field carries the same error. Impact: headword lookup on the name fails in both segments.",
