  "evidence": "QUOTED AS FILED BY THE RECONCILER: wylie/ACIP of C03:440 end at \"YANG YANG BSAMS PAS PHYI MA'I SNANG SHAS\" (no closing shad) while the row's English ends \"...And the cycle's suffering: stop desire for the future.\" || Confirmed from this row's own text. (1) The quoted verse is in nine-syllable metre: line 1 of the citation, las 'bras mi slu 'khor ba'i sdug bsngal rnams, has nine syllables; line 2, yang yang bsams pas phyi ma'i snang shas, has eight, so the row ends one syllable short of the line. (2) Every other verse line in the row closes with a shad (\", ,\"); the row ends at snang shas with none. (3) The row's English renders the complete line including its verb (\"stop desire for the future\"), and \"stop\" has no Tibetan anywhere in the row. The missing syllable is ldog: C03:441's wylie begins \"ldog,ces pa ltar thog mar 'khor ba'i phun tshogs...\" while its English begins \"Suppose at first we are able to find...\", so the 440/441 boundary is offset by one word. The undivided line is witnessed at C01:207 and C16:98 (\"...PHYI MA'I SNANG SHAS LDOG,CES PAS BSTAN TE\") and at P1:83 (\"...phyi ma'i snang shas ldog\", same source re-ingested, corroborative only). The same split recurs two rows later: C03:443 ends \"...yid la goms pas tshe 'di'i snang shas\" with English \"...Think it constantly, stop desire for this life.\" and C03:444 opens \"ldogs pa ltar...\". Effect: \"stop\" in this row is necessarily left unwrapped, and ldog in C03:441 has no English on its row. Nothing is lost across the pair, hence LOW. SKEPTIC PASS 2026-09-01 (two independent skeptics, one on each side of the boundary, both survived): C03:440's wylie, ACIP and raw ingest record stop at 'snang shas' and 'ldog,ces' opens 441; the independent witnesses C01:207 and C16:98 carry the undivided line '...phyi ma'i snang shas ldog,ces pas bstan te'. The mirror claim filed from 441 ('ldog,' as the tail of 440's verse) is the same defect and is folded into this record rather than billed twice. THE SAME CUT RECURS at eight C03 row boundaries: 36/37, 163/164, 165/166 (filed as E-062), 440/441, 443/444, 472/473, 594/595, 610/611 - a verse line's final syllable spilling into the next row is a class, not a one-off; recorded here as a list rather than as a class entry so every citation stays checkable.",
